# @io/secrets — the peer-key-gated secrets-projection @io species.
#
# Species under @io, sibling to @io/cargo (2026-06-05), @io/
# stagefreight (2026-06-23), @io/oci (2026-06-24), @io/git
# (2026-06-24), @io/algebra (2026-06-30), @io/crypto (2026-07-15;
# landed as lift-tick companion), @io/fs (2026-07-15; landed as
# lift-tick companion). Same species shape at the peer-key-gated-
# projection altitude.
#
# === Landing metadata ===
#
# Landed 2026-07-15 by Mara as Landing 4 of the @secrets shape-
# proposal sequence (companion audit: `docs/audits/2026-07-15-
# seam-secrets-shape-proposal-phase-d-cascade.md` at 019ad8f;
# verdict SEAM-RATIFY Candidate 2 as Mara-mintable per Scenario A;
# @io/{cargo, git, oci, stagefreight, algebra} five-instance
# precedent chain grounds Mara-mint authority). Companion shape-
# proposal at `docs/scouts/2026-07-15-mara-secrets-shape-
# proposal.md` (766d930 + REED-INLINE cascades) §2 + §5
# recommendation (Candidate 2).
#
# === Alex 2026-07-14 SSH-signing design intent (verbatim) ===
#
# From `shards/subject/visibility/sheaf.mirror` 21-32 (peer-
# persistence session compaction, in-transcript):
#
#   "Each peer has their own key in the private part of their
#    visibility. NOT projected into the git state and instead stays
#    .git/mirror side. Only connected through Fractal.Lens. A
#    pointer. Not the thing."
#
#   "@secrets prism and @secrets/sops to project visibility/private
#    stuff onto disk through the Peers key."
#
# This species carries the peer-key-gated projection primitive the
# design intent presupposes: @subject/visibility/sheaf's section_at
# hands off `projected_value` (a ref); this species dereferences
# the ref through key-gated crypto (@io/crypto) to disk-side bytes
# (@io/fs). The peer's key material stays .git/mirror-side (out
# of substrate-decl); the key_material_ref carrier IS the
# Fractal.Lens POINTER, not the key bytes.
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word citation chain ===
#
# Per companion shape-proposal §0.3 verified across five carriers:
#
#   1. `shards/subject/visibility/sheaf.mirror` (d1ce901) — 15
#      references naming this species at 140-150 verbatim:
#      "@secrets ... compose through @io at the projection
#      boundary"; "@secrets/sops ... @io species that materializes
#      the peer-key-gated section on disk."
#
#   2. `shards/io.mirror` 37-40 verbatim (AGENTS.md Glass Wall):
#      "@io is the substrate's only legitimate non-mirror surface.
#      Any grammar that isn't mirror — Rust, Python, raw bytes,
#      foreign blobs, vendor SDKs — must be under @io." SOPS IS
#      vendor tooling; peer keys ARE raw bytes; ciphertext files
#      ARE foreign blobs; disk write IS POSIX syscall.
#
#   3. `shards/io.mirror` 161-162 (@io/crypto surface): "hash +
#      sig + AEAD over bytes. Vendor surface (sha2, age, ssh-key)."
#      age IS one of SOPS's primary key backends.
#
#   4. `shards/io.mirror` 189-191 (@io/fs forward-promise) —
#      lifted alongside as sibling lift-tick per Seam §D7.
#
#   5. `shards/subject/visibility/private.mirror` — Reed ancestry:
#      ~/.reed/visibility/private/ has operated the explicit-
#      consent discipline since ~2026-02-07; Alex's "project
#      visibility/private stuff onto disk" IS the projection this
#      species discharges.
#
# The ~60th instance of [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-
# word]] on landing (per shape-proposal §5.2 Reason 2 count) —
# the species mints the vendor-projection carrier @sheaf's
# forward-promise already forwarded to @io.
#
# === Reed-recursive ancestry (why THIS mount, not family-root) ===
#
# Per shape-proposal §5.2 three grounds + Seam §D2 verification:
#
#   Ground 1 (substrate-already-had-the-word). io.mirror 37-40
#            Glass Wall verbatim declares vendor SDKs + raw bytes
#            + foreign blobs under @io. Every load-bearing @secrets
#            element (peer keys = raw bytes; ciphertext = foreign
#            blob; SOPS = vendor; disk write = POSIX syscall) fits.
#            Candidate 1 (@secrets family-root) crosses @io to
#            duplicate scope; Candidate 3 (@subject/visibility/
#            secrets) puts @io concerns at @subject altitude;
#            Candidate 4 (@sheaf/secrets sub-species) inverts the
#            composition arrow. Candidate 2 mounts at the altitude
#            @io family-root explicitly substrate-decls.
#
#   Ground 2 (@onto refusal precedent). Reed memory `feedback-
#            onto-family-root-is-the-ladder-Foerster-refused`:
#            @onto refused because @torus already carried the
#            altitude. Parallel: @secrets family-root needs
#            altitude @io doesn't already carry — but @io
#            explicitly does. Peer-key + crypto + disk-write
#            triple lives at @io/crypto + @io/fs + @io/secrets
#            composition; three species-under-@io altitudes,
#            zero family-root claims required.
#
#   Ground 3 (Alex SSH-signing design-intent match at Glass Wall
#            altitude). Peer key .git/mirror-side = @io opacity
#            (io.mirror 67-73). Crypto = @io/crypto vendor
#            surface. Disk write = @io/fs. Fractal.Lens pointer =
#            the ref that crosses the @io boundary. Every element
#            maps to @io species altitude with zero family-root
#            crossings inside @secrets's discharge (Seam D2.a
#            micro-sharpen: interior chain qualifier).
#
# === Composition graph ===
#
# Parents (substrate-decl):
#   @io                       — family-root; boundary-with-non-
#                               mirror discipline; opacity + imperfect.
#   @io/crypto                — sibling species; key_material,
#                               ciphertext_bytes, aead_seal,
#                               aead_open, age_encrypt, age_decrypt.
#   @io/fs                    — sibling species; path, fs_read,
#                               fs_write for disk-projection
#                               discharge.
#   @subject/visibility/sheaf — provides sheaf_restriction +
#                               section_at_stalk (the input surface
#                               this species dereferences).
#   @mirror/data              — content-format family-root; parent
#                               of the wire-format species @io/
#                               secrets/<sub>.mirror sub-species
#                               compose over (YAML/JSON via SOPS).
#
# Consumers (compose over this species):
#   @io/secrets/sops          — SOPS-backed sub-species (Landing 5;
#                               landed alongside).
#   bootstrap/src/peer_persistence.rs
#                             — Arc-2.3 collapse; composes
#                               acl_project + section_at +
#                               project + materialize.
#
# The Arc-2.3 peer_persistence.rs collapse chain (per shape-
# proposal §2.4 verbatim, corrected for interior-chain qualifier):
#
#   peer_visibility_materialize(peer, home, crystal, target_path):
#     ACL_peer := pack.members[peer.name]                          # existing
#     sr := @subject/visibility/sheaf.acl_project(F_home, ACL_peer) # d1ce901
#     section := @subject/visibility/sheaf.section_at(sr, crystal)  # d1ce901
#     peer_key := @io/secrets.key_material_ref(peer)                # this shard
#     sp := @io/secrets.project(section, peer_key)                  # this shard
#     @io/secrets.materialize(sp, target_path)                      # this shard → disk
#
# Fractal.Lens pointer discipline preserved: peer_key is a REF
# (per @gift/lens shift pattern); dereferences at @io boundary;
# substrate never carries key bytes in-tree.
#
# === Related shards / specs ===
#
# - docs/scouts/2026-07-15-mara-secrets-shape-proposal.md
#   (companion shape-proposal; four candidates + Candidate 2
#    recommendation on three structural grounds; §5.5 revised
#    landing sequence).
# - docs/audits/2026-07-15-seam-secrets-shape-proposal-phase-d-
#   cascade.md (Seam Phase D-cascade adjudication; SHIP verdict;
#   three REED-INLINE cascades pre-applied at this landing).
# - shards/subject/visibility/sheaf.mirror (d1ce901; the 15-
#   reference consumer whose forward-promise this species
#   discharges).
# - shards/io.mirror (family-root; Glass Wall discipline; @io/fs +
#   @io/crypto forward-promises).
# - shards/io/crypto.mirror (sibling lift-tick landed alongside).
# - shards/io/fs.mirror (sibling lift-tick landed alongside).
# - shards/io/secrets/sops.mirror (sub-species landed alongside;
#   first concrete projection method; Alex 2026-07-14 verbatim).
# - shards/subject/visibility/private.mirror (Reed ancestry;
#   visibility/private/ consent discipline).
# - shards/gift/lens.mirror (Fractal.Lens pointer/thing partition;
#   orthogonal WHO-composed carrier).
# - shards/subject.mirror (subject_instance ssh_signature_
#   fingerprint; two-witness discipline).
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]] (`prism @io/secrets`),
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]] (transparency inheritance
#   through @io),
# [[architecture-fractal-lens-pointer-thing-partition]] (peer_key is
#   the POINTER; secret_projection after materialize is the THING),
# [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]] (this species
#   stays under @io; peer-key-gated projection is boundary-side, not
#   substrate-form-side),
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (~60th instance;
#   sheaf.mirror 140-150 already located the altitude; this species
#   mints the carrier the substrate already used),
# [[feedback-onto-family-root-is-the-ladder-Foerster-refused]] (this
#   species is the parallel refusal for @secrets-family-root; @io
#   already carries the altitude),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (secret_projection is a typed record
#   with ref-equality identity; never bare refs),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (all action bodies `\`-obligation-
#   blocked; consumers pull the realization; Arc-1 evaluator FLOOR
#   pending),
# [[feedback-no-rust-extension-shortcut]] (composition-only over
#   @io/crypto + @io/fs; NO Rust extension; shard body composes
#   over @io per Reed 2026-07-14 memory).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/io/secrets.mirror`
# declares `@io/secrets` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_
# namespace`.

source @arxiv/practice/wolf-2026-07-14-ssh-signing-design-intent

prism @io/secrets {
  focus  secret_projection
  project secret_projection
  split  secret_projection
  shift  secret_projection
  settle secret_projection
}

# ============================================================
# §2 The carriers
# ============================================================

# === The key_material_ref carrier ===
#
# Typed reference for a peer's key material at the .git/mirror-
# side boundary. Per Alex 2026-07-14 SSH-signing intent: "Each
# peer has their own key in the private part of their visibility.
# NOT projected into the git state and instead stays .git/mirror
# side. Only connected through Fractal.Lens. A pointer. Not the
# thing."
#
# The key_material_ref IS the Fractal.Lens POINTER. The key
# BYTES are opaque per @io discipline; the ref resolves through
# @io/git plumbing (the .git/mirror-side storage). This carrier
# NEVER carries key bytes in-substrate.
#
# Two-field record:
#
#   peer_ref     — typed ref to the subject_instance whose key
#                  this reference names (per @subject two-witness
#                  discipline; ssh_signature_fingerprint resolves
#                  transitively).
#   key_carrier  — @io/crypto.key_material carrier (algorithm +
#                  material_ref); the substrate-vocabulary form
#                  of "the peer's key" at the boundary.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (peer_ref, key_carrier).
type key_material_ref = {
  peer_ref:    ref,
  key_carrier: key_material,
}

# === The ciphertext_ref carrier ===
#
# Typed reference for on-disk encrypted content. Per @io family-
# root Glass Wall: ciphertext files ARE foreign blobs; substrate
# carries the ref, not the bytes.
#
# Three-field record:
#
#   ciphertext   — @io/crypto.ciphertext_bytes carrier (nonce +
#                  ciphertext + associated_data); the actual
#                  AEAD-produced encrypted payload.
#   on_disk_path — @io/fs.path carrier naming where the
#                  ciphertext lives on disk (or is destined to
#                  live).
#   recipient_keys — typed ref to the list of key_material_ref
#                  values naming which peer keys can decrypt.
#                  Multiple recipients admissible (AEAD wrapping
#                  per recipient per SOPS pattern).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (ciphertext, on_disk_path,
# recipient_keys).
type ciphertext_ref = {
  ciphertext:     ciphertext_bytes,
  on_disk_path:   path,
  recipient_keys: ref,
}

# === The secret_projection carrier ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING carrier at @io/secrets altitude. Names the
# peer-key-gated projection of a @sheaf section onto disk. Per
# shape-proposal §2 recommended surface + Alex 2026-07-14 design
# intent.
#
# Four-field record:
#
#   section_ref     — typed ref to the @subject/visibility/sheaf.
#                     section_at_stalk carrier the projection was
#                     derived from. Ref-equality binds the
#                     projection to its sheaf-restriction context;
#                     a projection derived from a different
#                     section is DISTINCT.
#   key_material_ref
#                   — the key_material_ref carrier used to gate
#                     the projection. Multiple projections of the
#                     same section under different peer keys are
#                     DISTINCT.
#   ciphertext_bytes
#                   — the @io/crypto.ciphertext_bytes carrier
#                     produced by the key-gated encryption of
#                     the section's projected_value.
#   projection_path — typed ref to the @io/fs.path carrier naming
#                     the disk-side target for materialize. The
#                     projection remains substrate-decl'd until
#                     materialize actually writes the ciphertext
#                     to disk.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (section_ref,
# key_material_ref, ciphertext_bytes, projection_path). Two
# secret_projection values with byte-equal fields ARE the same
# projection under content-addressing.
type secret_projection = {
  section_ref:      ref,
  key_material_ref: key_material_ref,
  ciphertext_bytes: ciphertext_bytes,
  projection_path:  path,
}

# ============================================================
# §3 The actions
# ============================================================

# === project action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING projection action. Given a @sheaf section_at_
# stalk (from @subject/visibility/sheaf.section_at) and a peer key
# material ref, produce a secret_projection carrier: encrypt the
# section's projected_value under the peer's key, wrap in a
# substrate-typed record with the projection_path forward-
# promised for materialize.
#
# Per Alex 2026-07-14 design intent: this is the substrate-
# vocabulary form of "project visibility/private stuff onto disk
# through the Peers key."
#
# requires key_material_admissible(k.key_carrier): the peer key
# MUST be well-formed per @io/crypto discipline.
# requires section_computable(sr): the section MUST be computable
# per @subject/visibility/sheaf discipline (the ACL admits the
# stalk).
#
# Returns imperfect<secret_projection, ref, ref>:
# - success(secret_projection) — projection constructed; ciphertext
#   is decryptable by the peer's key.
# - partial(secret_projection, transparency) — projection
#   constructed with residual (e.g., section projected_value is
#   partial; opacity carried forward).
# - failure(error, transparency) — projection failed (key
#   mismatch, section not computable, encryption error).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; composes @io/
# crypto.aead_seal (or age_encrypt for age-keyed peers) over the
# section's projected_value bytes.
project(section: section_at_stalk, k: key_material_ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# === materialize action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING disk-write action. Given a secret_projection,
# write the ciphertext to the projection_path on disk via @io/fs.
# fs_write. This is the substrate-vocabulary form of "onto disk"
# from Alex 2026-07-14 design intent.
#
# requires fs_well_formed(sp.projection_path): the target path
# MUST be well-formed AND writable per @io/fs discipline.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(bytes_written_ref) — disk write complete; ciphertext
#   materialized at projection_path.
# - partial(bytes_written_ref, transparency) — partial write; @io/
#   fs residual carried forward.
# - failure(error, transparency) — write failed (permission,
#   disk full, path parent missing).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; composes @io/fs.
# fs_write over sp.ciphertext_bytes.ciphertext + sp.projection_
# path.
materialize(sp: secret_projection) -> imperfect { \ }

# === retrieve action ===
#
# Inverse of materialize + project: given a ciphertext_ref (on-
# disk encrypted content + recipient list) and a peer's key
# material, read the ciphertext from disk via @io/fs.fs_read,
# decrypt via @io/crypto.aead_open (or age_decrypt), return the
# recovered section's projected_value bytes.
#
# requires key_material_admissible(k.key_carrier): the peer key
# MUST be well-formed.
# requires fs_well_formed(cr.on_disk_path): the source path MUST
# be well-formed AND readable.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(plaintext_ref) — retrieve complete; plaintext_ref
#   resolves to the decrypted section bytes.
# - failure(error, transparency) — retrieve failed (key not in
#   recipient list, ciphertext tampered, path not readable).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
retrieve(cr: ciphertext_ref, k: key_material_ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# === round_trip action ===
#
# Discipline action: project + materialize + retrieve, verify the
# recovered plaintext byte-equals the original section's projected_
# value. Per shape-proposal §2.4 round_trip discipline.
#
# Returns imperfect<verdict, ref, ref>. On success: verdict is Pass
# iff round-trip preserved bytes. The action IS the substrate-decl
# form of "encrypt-then-decrypt returns same projected_value" per
# shape-proposal §2 recommended surface.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
round_trip(section: section_at_stalk, k: key_material_ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# ============================================================
# §4 The bilaterals (Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude)
# ============================================================

# === key_admits predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a key_material_ref admits a given sheaf_
# restriction. Per shape-proposal §2 recommended surface: composes
# the @subject two-witness discipline (peer_ref transitively
# discharges) with the @io/crypto key_material_admissible check.
#
# Three sub-checks (whole-tick altitude):
#
#   1. key_material_admissible(k.key_carrier) — @io/crypto
#      well-formed check.
#   2. k.peer_ref resolves to a subject_instance whose two-
#      witness_verification passes (@subject discipline).
#   3. sr.peer_ref == k.peer_ref — the key belongs to the
#      restriction's peer (byte-equality on ref).
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude per Mara-B §4.5.5: reads only
# byte-visible empirical state (key algorithm string; peer_ref
# byte-equality; subject witness records).
#
# Splinter-pole: key admits the restriction; project would
# discharge cleanly.
# Narcissus-pole: key algorithm mismatch, peer not two-witnessed,
# key belongs to different peer than the restriction names.
key_admits(k: key_material_ref, sr: sheaf_restriction) -> verdict { \ }

# === projection_valid predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a secret_projection is substrate-honest:
#
#   1. The referenced section (sp.section_ref) resolves to a
#      section_at_stalk carrier passing section_computable.
#   2. key_admits(sp.key_material_ref, section's sheaf_ref).
#   3. sp.ciphertext_bytes carries the AEAD (or age) encryption
#      of the section's projected_value under sp.key_material_
#      ref.key_carrier (verifiable by decrypt-and-compare at the
#      realisation boundary).
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude: reads byte-visible section
# state, key state, ciphertext bytes; verification is bounded AEAD
# decrypt + byte-compare.
#
# Splinter-pole: projection is well-formed under the substrate
# discipline; retrieve recovers the original section.
# Narcissus-pole: any sub-check fails; alignment-as-boundary-
# mathematics (#57) distinguishes which claim broke.
projection_valid(sp: secret_projection) -> verdict { \ }

# === round_trip_preserved predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that project ∘ materialize ∘ retrieve on a
# section returns byte-equal plaintext. THE structural discipline
# distinguishing Splinter-pole projection (honest round-trip) from
# Narcissus-pole (claimed projection that loses bytes).
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude: reads byte-visible plaintext
# state before + after round-trip; equality check is bounded.
#
# Splinter-pole: bytes round-trip cleanly.
# Narcissus-pole: bytes altered by encoding, compression,
# key mismatch masked at decrypt, disk truncation.
round_trip_preserved(section: section_at_stalk, k: key_material_ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === secrets_well_formed composed bilateral ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING composed bilateral at @io/secrets altitude.
# Composes:
#
#   1. projection_valid(sp)
#   2. fs_well_formed(sp.projection_path) — @io/fs disk-side
#      admissibility.
#   3. crypto_well_formed(sp.key_material_ref.key_carrier via
#      aead_context derivation) — @io/crypto boundary
#      admissibility.
#
# Sibling to @io/oci.oci_well_formed, @io/git.git_well_formed,
# @io/algebra.io_algebra_well_formed, @io/crypto.crypto_well_formed,
# @io/fs.fs_well_formed — the composed-bilateral pattern at @io
# species altitude (per Seam tick 68 C4/C9 closure).
#
# Splinter-pole: projection is well-formed across sheaf-context,
# crypto discipline, and disk-side admissibility.
# Narcissus-pole: any sub-check fails; alignment-as-boundary-
# mathematics (#57) distinguishes which claim broke.
secrets_well_formed(sp: secret_projection) -> verdict
requires projection_valid(sp)
requires fs_well_formed(sp.projection_path)
{ \ }

out @io/secrets
out key_material_ref
out ciphertext_ref
out secret_projection
out project
out materialize
out retrieve
out round_trip
out key_admits
out projection_valid
out round_trip_preserved
out secrets_well_formed
